\textbf{E1, equal budget.} The four alpha-beta agents with a sensible evaluation (hand-crafted, TDLeaf, GA, constrained GA) are within <<spread>> Elo of each other and their intervals overlap (about $\pm$<<ci>> Elo), so we cannot rank them. The AlphaZero-style agent is <<elo_Z>> Elo below the hand-crafted agent (95\% interval <<lo_Z>> to <<hi_Z>>; score <<sc_ZH>> against it, <<wdl_ZH>>), the equal-weight agent is <<elo_Q>>, and UCT MCTS is <<elo_M>>. TDLeaf starts from equal piece values and no positional knowledge, which is the equal-weight agent; the \emph{learned} weights are worth <<td_gain>> Elo more. The GA, which starts from random weights, ends at <<elo_G>> (unconstrained) and <<elo_C>> (constrained).

\textbf{Cost.} A simulation of the network agent is much more expensive than an alpha-beta node: <<ms_Z>> ms against <<ms_H>> ms per move at $B=400$. When alpha-beta is given the <<eqt_budget>> nodes it can search in the same time, the network agent scores only <<eqt_score>> against it (<<eqt_wdl>>); at that budget alpha-beta reaches depth <<depth_eq>> instead of <<depth_400>>. UCT MCTS needs <<ms_M>> ms per move and still scores <<eqt_m_score>> against time-matched alpha-beta.

\textbf{E2, external opponent.} (Fig.~\ref{fig:a}b.) The hand-crafted, GA and constrained-GA agents score <<ext_top_low_min>>--<<ext_top_low_max>> against Fairy-Stockfish at depths 1--2, hold roughly parity at depths 3--4 and score about <<ext_top_high_min>>--<<ext_top_high_max>> at depths 10--12. The AlphaZero-style and TDLeaf agents follow the same curve somewhat lower at shallow depth. The equal-weight agent averages <<ext_eq>> over depths 1--4 and the TDLeaf agent trained from it <<ext_td>> (hand-crafted: <<ext_hand>>); MCTS and the greedy agent score at most <<ext_bad_max>> even at depth 1.

\textbf{E3, learning curves.} (Fig.~\ref{fig:a}c.) All three learners start far below the hand-crafted agent and climb towards it. TDLeaf goes from <<td_first>> (equal weights) to about <<td_plateau>> within roughly 2400 games and then fluctuates; the GA climbs from <<ga_first>> to about <<ga_plateau>> within 4000 fitness games; the network agent rises from <<az_first>> to about <<az_plateau>> after roughly 7000 self-play games and then stays flat (policy loss 1.37). None settles above about 0.6.

\textbf{E4, move quality.} (Table~\ref{tab:main}.) The constrained GA, GA and hand-crafted agents lose <<loss_C>>, <<loss_G>> and <<loss_H>> cp per move and TDLeaf <<loss_T>>; their paired differences from the hand-crafted agent have bootstrap intervals that include zero. The equal-weight agent loses <<loss_Q>> cp and the AlphaZero-style agent <<loss_Z>> cp (<<pd_Z>> cp more than hand-crafted, 95\% interval <<pd_Z_ci>>); its blunder rate is <<bl_Z>>\% against <<bl_H>>\%.

\textbf{Learned weights and scaling.} (Fig.~\ref{fig:b}.) TDLeaf values knight, bishop, rook and queen at <<tv_N>>, <<tv_B>>, <<tv_R>> and <<tv_Q>> pawns (hand-crafted: 3, 3.2, 5, 9); the unconstrained GA at <<gv_N>>, <<gv_B>>, <<gv_R>> and <<gv_Q>>. The order is right but the scale is compressed. Strength grows with budget for every agent: the network agent gains from <<scal_Z0>> to <<scal_Z1>> Elo between 25 and 1600 simulations (measured against alpha-beta at 400 nodes), and alpha-beta with the GA weights reaches <<scal_G1600>> points at 1600 nodes; MCTS stays below <<scal_Mmax>> points against that opponent and is omitted from the plot.

