\section{Discussion}
\textbf{Learning recovers, but does not exceed, expert knowledge.} Two methods that were given no chess knowledge end close to a hand-written evaluation: the GA, from game scores inside its population, and TDLeaf, from the result signal (somewhat lower than the hand-crafted agent against the outside engine at shallow depth); the network agent, trained on search statistics, ends further behind. The GA and TDLeaf match the hand-crafted agent in move quality (E4). The learned piece values are smaller than the textbook ones. The GA tuned its weights at 400 nodes, where alpha-beta reaches depth about <<depth_400>> (mean over our E4 positions), so part of what they encode may be a correction for shallow search; TDLeaf trained with depth-3 search. We did not test this, or the effect of board size.

\textbf{Why the AlphaZero-style agent is behind.} With 12\,800 games and a 216\,000-parameter perceptron, the network is a weaker evaluator than 14 hand-chosen features combined with exact tactics from quiescence search; its losses stop decreasing early, which suggests a limit of data or capacity rather than of training time, but we did not vary either. Its scaling curve is the steepest, so it is not hopeless, but each simulation costs about <<cost_ratio>> nodes. The result should not be read as a statement about AlphaZero at scale.

\textbf{UCT MCTS} is limited by random rollouts: in a tactical game a single blunder decides the result, so value estimates are noisy and our budgets are small.

\textbf{A validity lesson.} In an earlier version of the harness both sides of a game shared one transposition table, so an agent could reuse scores computed with the other agent's weights. In that version the unconstrained GA scored 0.74 against the hand-crafted agent at 2000 nodes (now <<ga_hand2000>>), and the AlphaZero-style agent looked like the strongest one. The inconsistency showed up only against Fairy-Stockfish: an agent that beat the hand-crafted one head to head scored near zero from depth 4. Each side now owns its table, and every number here was produced after the fix. Without an external opponent we might not have seen it.

\section{Inference}
The evidence supports: (1) game results alone are enough for TDLeaf to move an equal-weight evaluation by <<td_gain>> Elo to hand-crafted level, and a GA reaches the same level from random weights; no learner is shown to be better than the hand-written weights, since all intervals overlap; (2) at this data and compute scale a search with a linear evaluation beats a small self-play network both at equal budget and at equal time, and UCT with random rollouts is far weaker; (3) the in-pool ranking agrees with an independent engine and with move-level analysis, which is why we trust it. It does not support: differences smaller than about $\pm$<<ci>> Elo; claims about seeds, since each learner was trained once; claims about larger networks, longer training, or other variants. The E4 positions and Fairy-Stockfish depth are proxies, not a calibrated Elo.

\section{Conclusion}
We compared five techniques for Gardner minichess on one engine under one compute currency. With the same alpha-beta search, an evaluation learned by TDLeaf or evolved by a GA from no knowledge reaches the level of a hand-written one; an AlphaZero-style network trained for 35 CPU-minutes does not, and costs more per simulation; random-rollout MCTS is far behind. External play against Fairy-Stockfish was essential to detect a harness error. Next steps are repeated seeds with confidence bands, GA fitness that includes an outside opponent or a hall of fame \cite{rosin1997}, convolutional networks with search-value targets, and the Silverman $4\times5$ and Los Alamos $6\times6$ variants to test transfer. Code, data and the interactive board are in the project repository.
